---
import ProjectLayout from '../../layouts/ProjectLayout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
---
<ProjectLayout
  title="Diagnosing Intermittent Web-Map Rendering Failures"
  year="2026"
  section="GIS Project Management"
  category="Production Incident Investigation · ZanSDI / Sivan Design"
  tagline="An observability and root-cause analysis workflow for a production GeoNode / GeoServer SDI platform — built with synthetic monitoring, Grafana, Loki and AI-assisted log analysis."
  tools={['Python', 'Playwright', 'PostgreSQL', 'dbt', 'Grafana', 'Loki', 'GitHub Actions', 'GeoServer', 'GeoFence']}
>

  <section class="proj-section">
    <h2 class="proj-section-title">Project Summary</h2>
    <div class="proj-body">
      <p>
        The production web-map on <strong>ZanSDI</strong>, Zanzibar's national Spatial Data
        Infrastructure platform, occasionally failed to render — silently, intermittently, and
        without correlating to load, server health or any obvious infrastructure signal. This case
        study documents how we investigated and resolved it: by building a purpose-made observability
        stack around synthetic user monitoring and centralized log analysis, rather than relying on
        standard uptime checks that had no way of detecting the failure.
      </p>
      <p>
        The investigation traced the problem to stale, silently-dead database connections being
        reused by <strong>GeoFence</strong>, GeoServer's authorization component — and closed it out
        with configuration changes validated against the same monitoring pipeline that found it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="proj-section">
    <h2 class="proj-section-title">The Production Problem</h2>
    <div class="proj-body">
      <p>
        ZanSDI is built on <strong>GeoNode</strong> and <strong>GeoServer</strong>. Its production
        web-map began exhibiting intermittent rendering failures with a frustrating profile: real,
        user-facing, and almost impossible to pin down.
      </p>
    </div>
    <ul class="proj-list">
      <li>Map layers occasionally failed to render.</li>
      <li>Some map requests stayed pending for a long time before resolving or timing out.</li>
      <li>The issue occurred randomly and could not be reproduced on demand.</li>
      <li>It did not correlate with the number of active users.</li>
      <li>CPU, memory and general server health metrics looked normal throughout.</li>
      <li>The platform itself remained online and reachable at all times.</li>
      <li>Traditional infrastructure monitoring showed no obvious failures.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="proj-callout">
      <p>
        <strong>Key insight:</strong> the application stayed <em>available</em> while individual
        rendering requests <em>silently failed</em>. Uptime monitoring answers "is the service up?"
        — it has no way of answering "did this specific map request actually render?" That gap is
        what made the problem invisible to standard monitoring and is what the investigation had to
        solve for first.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="proj-section">
    <h2 class="proj-section-title">Monitoring Architecture</h2>
    <div class="proj-body">
      <p>
        We designed an ad-hoc observability solution combining infrastructure monitoring with
        synthetic user monitoring, so that every rendering failure would leave behind precise,
        correlated evidence: what failed, exactly when, and what the system was doing at that moment.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h3 class="proj-flow-label">Synthetic monitoring &amp; analytics pipeline</h3>
    <div class="proj-flow" role="img" aria-label="Flow: a synthetic user exercises GeoNode, GeoServer and GeoFence, raw observations are stored in PostgreSQL, dbt transforms them into analytical models, and Grafana dashboards visualize the results">
      <span class="proj-flow-step">Synthetic user</span>
      <span class="proj-flow-arrow">→</span>
      <span class="proj-flow-step">GeoNode / GeoServer<br /><small>/ GeoFence</small></span>
      <span class="proj-flow-arrow">→</span>
      <span class="proj-flow-step">Raw observations<br /><small>PostgreSQL</small></span>
      <span class="proj-flow-arrow">→</span>
      <span class="proj-flow-step">dbt<br /><small>transformations</small></span>
      <span class="proj-flow-arrow">→</span>
      <span class="proj-flow-step">Grafana<br /><small>dashboards</small></span>
    </div>

    <h3 class="proj-flow-label">Log aggregation pipeline</h3>
    <div class="proj-flow" role="img" aria-label="Flow: application and system logs are centralized in Loki and visualized on a Grafana log dashboard">
      <span class="proj-flow-step">Application &amp;<br /><small>system logs</small></span>
      <span class="proj-flow-arrow">→</span>
      <span class="proj-flow-step">Loki</span>
      <span class="proj-flow-arrow">→</span>
      <span class="proj-flow-step">Grafana<br /><small>log dashboard</small></span>
    </div>

    <h3 class="proj-flow-label">Investigation → resolution</h3>
    <div class="proj-flow" role="img" aria-label="Flow: synthetic monitoring, logs and AI-assisted analysis combine to identify the root cause, leading to GeoFence configuration improvements">
      <span class="proj-flow-step">Synthetic monitoring</span>
      <span class="proj-flow-plus">+</span>
      <span class="proj-flow-step">Logs</span>
      <span class="proj-flow-plus">+</span>
      <span class="proj-flow-step">AI-assisted analysis</span>
      <span class="proj-flow-arrow">→</span>
      <span class="proj-flow-step">Root cause<br /><small>identification</small></span>
      <span class="proj-flow-arrow">→</span>
      <span class="proj-flow-step">GeoFence config<br /><small>improvements</small></span>
    </div>

    <div class="proj-body">
      <p>
        On the infrastructure side, system and application logs were centralized with
        <strong>Loki</strong> and wired into Grafana alongside the existing monitoring stack. The
        objective was narrow and specific: preserve evidence from exactly the moments in which
        rendering problems occurred, so that a failure captured at 03:14 could be traced back to the
        log lines emitted at 03:14 — not inferred after the fact from aggregate metrics.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="proj-section">
    <h2 class="proj-section-title">Synthetic Monitoring Workflow</h2>
    <div class="proj-body">
      <p>
        The core of the investigation was an automated synthetic user that behaved like a real
        person using the map — repeatedly, continuously, and with every observation logged. Each
        monitoring cycle:
      </p>
    </div>
    <ul class="proj-list">
      <li>Opened the production web map.</li>
      <li>Executed both anonymous and authenticated scenarios.</li>
      <li>Zoomed through a predefined set of map scales.</li>
      <li>Panned around the map to trigger new tile and feature requests.</li>
      <li>Issued real WMS and tile requests against the live GeoServer instance.</li>
      <li>Measured response times for every request.</li>
      <li>Recorded each request as successful, failed or pending.</li>
      <li>Captured a screenshot whenever a rendering problem occurred.</li>
      <li>Stored every observation in PostgreSQL for downstream analysis.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="proj-body">
      <p>
        A <strong>dbt</strong> analytics layer transformed the raw observation rows into analytical
        models — cycle-level rollups, success-rate windows, response-time distributions — that fed
        Grafana directly. The resulting dashboards visualized:
      </p>
    </div>
    <ul class="proj-list">
      <li>Monitoring cycle status (successful vs. failed) over time.</li>
      <li>Success rate over time.</li>
      <li>Failed requests.</li>
      <li>Pending requests.</li>
      <li>Average response time.</li>
      <li>Historical monitoring cycles, for pattern-spotting across days.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="proj-body">
      <p>
        The whole pipeline ran automatically and continuously via <strong>GitHub Actions</strong>,
        so monitoring didn't depend on anyone manually kicking off a check — it was always running,
        always writing evidence, whether or not anyone was watching Grafana at the time.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="proj-section">
    <h2 class="proj-section-title">Investigation Process</h2>
    <div class="proj-body">
      <p>
        The monitoring ran continuously for several days. The diagnosis came from correlating three
        complementary sources of evidence, not from any single error message:
      </p>
    </div>

    <ol class="proj-timeline">
      <li class="proj-timeline-item">
        <span class="proj-timeline-num">1</span>
        <div class="proj-timeline-content">
          <h4>Synthetic monitoring results</h4>
          <p>The synthetic monitoring pipeline precisely identified the timestamps at which rendering failures occurred — down to the specific cycle, request and scenario.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li class="proj-timeline-item">
        <span class="proj-timeline-num">2</span>
        <div class="proj-timeline-content">
          <h4>Centralized log correlation</h4>
          <p>Those timestamps were cross-referenced against the Grafana log dashboards fed by Loki, surfacing GeoServer, GeoFence and infrastructure log activity around each failure window.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li class="proj-timeline-item">
        <span class="proj-timeline-num">3</span>
        <div class="proj-timeline-content">
          <h4>AI-assisted log analysis</h4>
          <p>AI-assisted analysis was used to inspect large volumes of correlated logs and identify recurring patterns across multiple, independent failure windows — patterns that were easy to miss when reading logs manually one incident at a time.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li class="proj-timeline-item">
        <span class="proj-timeline-num">4</span>
        <div class="proj-timeline-content">
          <h4>Convergent diagnosis</h4>
          <p>Rather than relying on a single log line or stack trace, the root cause emerged from the intersection of all three sources — the same underlying pattern showing up independently in the monitoring data, the logs and the AI-assisted review.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="proj-section">
    <h2 class="proj-section-title">Monitoring in Practice</h2>
    <img
      src={`${base}/maps/geofence-rendering-investigation-1.png`}
      alt="Grafana dashboard showing JVM process status and memory metrics — uptime, CPU cores, resident memory, heap used, live threads and heap/memory pool time series — monitored alongside GeoServer and GeoFence logs during the investigation"
      class="proj-map"
      loading="lazy"
    />
    <p class="proj-map-caption">
      Centralized Grafana and Loki log analysis used to correlate GeoServer, GeoFence and
      infrastructure events during rendering failures.
    </p>

    <img
      src={`${base}/maps/geofence-rendering-investigation-2.png`}
      alt="Grafana dashboard showing the Synthetic Monitoring Cycle Status timeline with green bars for successful cycles and red bars for failed cycles, a Success Rate Over Time chart, and summary panels for successful, pending and failed requests plus average response time"
      class="proj-map"
      loading="lazy"
    />
    <p class="proj-map-caption">
      Synthetic monitoring dashboard showing monitoring cycles over time. Green bars indicate
      successful rendering sessions while red bars indicate monitoring cycles in which one or more
      expected rendering requests failed.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="proj-section">
    <h2 class="proj-section-title">Root Cause</h2>
    <div class="proj-body">
      <p>
        The investigation traced the intermittent rendering failures to <strong>GeoFence</strong>,
        the authorization component sitting in front of GeoServer. GeoFence reused stale database
        connections from its connection pool over the network. Some of those connections still
        appeared alive at the pool level but were no longer actually usable.
      </p>
      <p>
        When a stale connection was handed out and reused, the request holding it would wait —
        sometimes for several minutes — before the connection finally timed out. Because this only
        happened when a specific unusable connection was drawn from the pool, it explained every
        symptom observed:
      </p>
    </div>
    <ul class="proj-list">
      <li>Why rendering occasionally stalled rather than failing outright.</li>
      <li>Why users experienced missing map layers instead of a clear error.</li>
      <li>Why infrastructure monitoring still looked healthy — CPU, memory and uptime were never affected, only individual requests stuck behind a dead connection.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="proj-section">
    <h2 class="proj-section-title">Resolution</h2>
    <div class="proj-body">
      <p>
        The fix targeted GeoFence's database connection handling directly, so that requests could no
        longer block indefinitely on a connection that looked alive but wasn't:
      </p>
    </div>
    <ul class="proj-list">
      <li>Test database connections before using them, rather than trusting pool state alone.</li>
      <li>Periodically remove stale idle connections from the pool.</li>
      <li>Enable TCP network keepalive so dead connections are detected sooner.</li>
      <li>Stop database connection attempts after 5 seconds.</li>
      <li>Stop waiting for database responses after 30 seconds.</li>
      <li>Stop waiting for an available pooled connection after 10 seconds.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="proj-body">
      <p>
        Together, these changes prevented requests from blocking on a database connection that could
        never actually complete — replacing an unbounded, silent stall with a bounded, fast failure
        that the connection pool could recover from.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="proj-section">
    <h2 class="proj-section-title">Outcome</h2>
    <ul class="proj-list">
      <li>Synthetic monitoring continued running unchanged after the fix, using the same pipeline that had originally detected the problem.</li>
      <li>The previously observed intermittent rendering failures did not reoccur during the validation period.</li>
      <li>Grafana dashboards confirmed stable rendering behaviour across the monitored window.</li>
      <li>The investigation produced a reusable monitoring framework — synthetic monitoring, log correlation and AI-assisted analysis — rather than a one-time troubleshooting effort.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="proj-note">
      <h3 class="proj-note-title">On the outcome claim</h3>
      <p>
        We don't claim this class of issue can never occur again. What the monitoring demonstrated is
        stable, failure-free rendering behaviour throughout the monitored validation period —
        measured the same way the original problem was found, not just asserted after the fact.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="proj-section">
    <h2 class="proj-section-title">Technologies</h2>
    <div class="proj-stack-grid">
      <div class="proj-stack-group">
        <h3 class="proj-stack-label">Synthetic Monitoring</h3>
        <ul class="proj-stack-list">
          <li>Python</li>
          <li>Playwright</li>
          <li>GitHub Actions</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="proj-stack-group">
        <h3 class="proj-stack-label">Data Pipeline</h3>
        <ul class="proj-stack-list">
          <li>PostgreSQL</li>
          <li>Neon PostgreSQL</li>
          <li>SQL</li>
          <li>dbt</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="proj-stack-group">
        <h3 class="proj-stack-label">Observability</h3>
        <ul class="proj-stack-list">
          <li>Grafana</li>
          <li>Loki</li>
          <li>AI-assisted log analysis</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="proj-stack-group">
        <h3 class="proj-stack-label">GIS Platform</h3>
        <ul class="proj-stack-list">
          <li>GeoNode</li>
          <li>GeoServer</li>
          <li>GeoFence</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="proj-section">
    <h2 class="proj-section-title">My Role</h2>
    <div class="proj-body">
      <p>I led the observability and monitoring side of the investigation end-to-end:</p>
    </div>
    <ul class="proj-list">
      <li>Monitoring strategy and overall investigation design.</li>
      <li>Synthetic monitoring design and automated implementation.</li>
      <li>Data pipeline design, from raw observations to analytical models.</li>
      <li>PostgreSQL data model for monitoring observations.</li>
      <li>dbt analytical models transforming raw observations into dashboard-ready data.</li>
      <li>Grafana dashboard development and SQL analytics.</li>
      <li>AI-assisted log analysis.</li>
      <li>Correlating monitoring evidence with application and infrastructure logs.</li>
      <li>Technical investigation and diagnosis, working jointly with the GeoServer/GeoFence side of the effort.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="proj-section">
    <h2 class="proj-section-title">Collaboration</h2>
    <div class="proj-collab">
      <p>
        This investigation and monitoring solution was developed collaboratively. I worked together
        with <strong>Mushegh Mkrtchyan</strong>, GIS Developer at Sivan Design, on the investigation,
        monitoring implementation and resolution of the rendering issue.
      </p>
      <p>
        Mushegh contributed to the GeoServer, GeoFence and GIS platform investigation and
        implementation, while I led the monitoring strategy, synthetic user framework, automation,
        PostgreSQL and dbt analytics pipeline, Grafana dashboards, analytical modelling and
        AI-assisted log analysis. The diagnosis and resolution were achieved through close technical
        collaboration between the two sides of the investigation.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="proj-section">
    <h2 class="proj-section-title">Skills Demonstrated</h2>
    <div class="proj-skills">
      <span class="proj-skill">Production incident investigation</span>
      <span class="proj-skill">Observability engineering</span>
      <span class="proj-skill">Synthetic monitoring</span>
      <span class="proj-skill">Root cause analysis</span>
      <span class="proj-skill">GIS platform engineering</span>
      <span class="proj-skill">GeoServer administration</span>
      <span class="proj-skill">Data engineering</span>
      <span class="proj-skill">SQL analytics</span>
      <span class="proj-skill">dbt</span>
      <span class="proj-skill">Grafana dashboard design</span>
      <span class="proj-skill">Log analysis</span>
      <span class="proj-skill">AI-assisted diagnostics</span>
      <span class="proj-skill">Cross-system troubleshooting</span>
      <span class="proj-skill">Technical leadership</span>
      <span class="proj-skill">Monitoring architecture</span>
    </div>
  </section>

</ProjectLayout>

